C = [
("h1", "1. Preface: The Question and the Position"),
("p", "This volume argues a position. It does not gather what churches say about the end of the lost and leave the reader to choose. It says what the author believes Scripture reveals, it sets the verses in the order they stand, and it answers the strongest objections from named modern writers, so that a reader who disagrees knows where the disagreement lies."),
("p", "The question is old and it is asked in earnest. Three answers are given to it among those who hold that Scripture is the word of God. One holds that the lost are punished without end and are conscious in it. One holds that the lost are destroyed, and that destruction means they cease to exist, either at death or after a period of punishment. This second view goes by the names annihilationism and conditional immortality, and it is held today by a number of evangelical writers who are careful with the text. A third holds that all will at last be reconciled. This volume takes up the first two. It does not take up the third."),
("p", "The position is this. The words Scripture uses for the end of the lost are not one word, and none of them settles the question by itself. Death, destruction, perishing, punishment, torment, exclusion, the second death and judgment each stand in their own passage with their own audience, and each has to be read there. A word that sometimes describes a thing coming to an end also describes, in other verses, a thing that remains in being and is ruined. Read cumulatively and each in its place, the passages that speak of final judgment tell of a punishment that cannot be reversed and does not end, and where the text says so, of an experience that continues. They do not require that the lost cease to exist."),
("p", "Three things about the method. First, the King James Version is the volume’s base English text, and every Scripture quotation is checked word for word against it. Where lexical or grammatical questions affect the argument, the relevant Hebrew or Greek wording is examined without replacing the passage’s context with a word-study argument. Second, each chapter opens with a claim, reads its passages in order, and closes with a short table and a list of what the volume takes from the passages, so that the reader can see what has been argued and test it. Third, where a modern writer is named, the volume says what that writer holds, in its own words, and gives the writer’s words only where it has read them in a free full text."),
("p", "The volume does not take up everything its neighbours argue. Questions concerning the distinct courts of Scripture are treated fully in Volume 5 of the Prophecy & Mystery Commentary Series, Separate Courts, and in Studies 31, 49 and 68. This volume does not reproduce that complete architecture, but chapter 8 examines the great white throne to the extent necessary to identify the company under judgment and interpret the lake of fire and the second death. The words for the unseen world and the lake of fire, reported as written, belong to Study 79, which takes no position and is not changed by this volume. The account in Luke 16 belongs to Parable Study 24, and the Lamb and the foundation of the world to Study 80. On those matters this volume cites the places and does not repeat them."),
("p", "One boundary runs through the volume. The passages are read in the settings in which they stand. The Lord’s words in the Gospels were spoken to Israel in His Kingdom ministry, and the visions of Revelation name the audience they were sent to. Neither is carried over to the Body of Christ, and nothing in this volume is a warning to the Body or a statement about its standing. What those passages reveal about the fate of the wicked is the evidence the volume uses. It distinguishes that testimony from any application to the Body."),
("sub", "The Thesis"),
("p", "Destruction language in Scripture does not by itself establish that the lost cease to exist. Its meaning is fixed by context, and the words for death, destruction, punishment, torment, exclusion, the second death and judgment are not one word. Taken together, each in its own setting, the passages on final judgment establish a punishment that is irreversible and enduring, and where the text says so, a continuing experience of it. They do not require cessation of existence. No single Greek word and no single proof text carries the case, and the volume does not rest it on one."),
("ctbl", "The thesis in five statements.", [
    ("Statement", "What it says", "Where it is argued"),
    ("1", "The words for destruction and perishing describe an ending in some verses and a ruined condition of something that remains in others. The word alone does not decide.", "Chapters 2 and 3"),
    ("2", "Where the Lord speaks of the power to destroy soul and body in hell, the text states a power, in a mission to Israel, and does not state an outcome.", "Chapter 4"),
    ("3", "Everlasting destruction from the presence of the Lord is read from its context as an irreversible judicial penalty; the preposition may name exclusion or source, and the verse does not by itself state continuing experience.", "Chapter 5"),
    ("4", "The same word for everlasting is set on the life and on the punishment in one verse, and the passages that follow describe a fire and a worm that do not end.", "Chapters 6 and 7"),
    ("5", "In Revelation the worshippers of the beast are tormented and have no rest day nor night, the plural statement of torment at 20:10 takes in the beast and the false prophet cast in a thousand years before, and the second death is described by what the lake contains and does.", "Chapter 8"),
  ], [0.16, 0.62, 0.22]),
("p", "A reader may test the thesis in one move. Take any verse in which a word of destruction stands, and ask three questions of it: who is addressed, what has just been said, and what does the verse say follows. The volume holds that the answers decide the meaning and that the word alone does not. If a passage can be produced in which the context itself states that the person ceases to be, the thesis is tested at that point, and the volume says in chapter 9 which passages come nearest to that and why it reads them as it does."),
]
